@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1317 = private unnamed_addr constant [795 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `str.join` calls that can be replaced with f-strings.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0Af-strings are more readable and generally preferred over `str.join` calls.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0A\22 \22.join((foo, bar))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Af\22{foo} {bar}\22\0A```\0A\0A# Fix safety\0AThe fix is always marked unsafe because the evaluation of the f-string\0Aexpressions will default to calling the `__format__` method of each\0Aobject, whereas `str.join` expects each object to be an instance of\0A`str` and uses the corresponding string. Therefore it is possible for\0Athe values of the resulting strings to differ, or for one expression\0Ato raise an exception while the other does not.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: f-strings](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/lexical_analysis.html#f-strings)\0A", align 1
