@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1423 = private unnamed_addr constant [859 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `__index__` implementations that return non-integer values.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `__index__` method should return an integer. Returning a different\0Atype may cause unexpected behavior.\0A\0ANote: `bool` is a subclass of `int`, so it's technically valid for `__index__` to\0Areturn `True` or `False`. However, a `DeprecationWarning` (`DeprecationWarning:\0A__index__ returned non-int (type bool)`) for such cases was already introduced,\0Athus this is a conscious difference between the original pylint rule and the\0Acurrent ruff implementation.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def __index__(self):\0A        return \222\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def __index__(self):\0A        return 2\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: The `__index__` method](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#object.__index__)\0A", align 1
