@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1470 = private unnamed_addr constant [985 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `super` calls that pass redundant arguments.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python 3, `super` can be invoked without any arguments when: (1) the\0Afirst argument is `__class__`, and (2) the second argument is equivalent to\0Athe first argument of the enclosing method.\0A\0AWhen possible, omit the arguments to `super` to make the code more concise\0Aand maintainable.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass A:\0A    def foo(self):\0A        pass\0A\0A\0Aclass B(A):\0A    def bar(self):\0A        super(B, self).foo()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass A:\0A    def foo(self):\0A        pass\0A\0A\0Aclass B(A):\0A    def bar(self):\0A        super().foo()\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe because removing the arguments from a call\0Amay delete comments that are attached to the arguments.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `super`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#super)\0A- [super/MRO, Python's most misunderstood feature.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1PQ7zzltz4)\0A", align 1
