@anon.8ad231a129453d088979dfad2582f60a.1478 = private unnamed_addr constant [1001 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `int` with an explicit base in which a string expression\0Ais stripped of its leading prefix (i.e., `0b`, `0o`, or `0x`).\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AGiven an integer string with a prefix (e.g., `0xABC`), Python can automatically\0Adetermine the base of the integer by the prefix without needing to specify\0Ait explicitly.\0A\0AInstead of `int(num[2:], 16)`, use `int(num, 0)`, which will automatically\0Adeduce the base based on the prefix.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Anum = \220xABC\22\0A\0Aif num.startswith(\220b\22):\0A    i = int(num[2:], 2)\0Aelif num.startswith(\220o\22):\0A    i = int(num[2:], 8)\0Aelif num.startswith(\220x\22):\0A    i = int(num[2:], 16)\0A\0Aprint(i)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Anum = \220xABC\22\0A\0Ai = int(num, 0)\0A\0Aprint(i)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThe rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as Ruff cannot guarantee that the\0Aargument to `int` will remain valid when its base is included in the\0Afunction call.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `int`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#int)\0A", align 1
